<h2>Profile</h2>

<p><strong>Full name:</strong> Eithne Patricia Ní Bhraonáin</p> 
<p><strong>Birthday:</strong> 17 May 1961</p>
<p><strong>Place of birth:</strong> Dore, Gweedore (Gaoth Dobhair), County Donegal, Ireland</p>
<p><strong>Parents:</strong> Leopold "Leo" and Máire "Baba" Brennan</p>
<p><strong>Siblings:</strong> Four brothers (Ciarán, Pól, Leon, Bartley) and four sisters (Máire/Moya, Deirdre, Olive, Brídín)</p>
<p><strong>Mother language:</strong> Irish Gaelic</p>

<h3 id="Musical_upbringing_and_Clannad">Musical upbringing and Clannad</h2>

<p>Eithne Patricia Ní Bhraonáin was the fifth of nine children born into a highly artistic family.</p> 

<p>Her maternal grandparents performed in a band throughout Ireland, deeply rooting her in a musical heritage.</p> 

<p>Her father, <strong>Leo Brennan</strong>, led the Slieve Foy showband before purchasing a local pub in 1968, famously naming it <strong>Leo's Tavern</strong>.</p> 

<p>Her mother, <strong>Máire</strong>, was a former dance band musician who became a music teacher and choir director at the local comprehensive school.</p> 

<p>At age 11, Enya attended <strong>Loreto College</strong>, a strict convent boarding school in Milford, paid for by her grandfather.</p> 

<p>There, she developed a profound love for classical music, art, Latin, and watercolor painting.</p>

<h3>Joining the Family Band</h3>

<p>In <strong>1980</strong>, instead of pursuing a university music degree, Enya accepted an invitation to join her family's Celtic folk band, <a href="/enya-clannad/">Clannad</a>.</p> 

<p>The band already featured her older siblings Máire (Moya), Pól, and Ciarán, alongside her twin uncles Noel and Pádraig Duggan.</p> 

<p>Invited by Clannad's sound engineer and manager, <strong>Nicky Ryan</strong>, Enya provided backing vocals and played the Wurlitzer electric piano and Prophet 5 synthesizer.</p> 

<p>She contributed to two of Clannad's albums: <em>Crann Úll</em> (1980) and <em>Fuaim</em> (1981).</p> 

<p>Despite touring Europe, Enya was considered a "hired hand" rather than a permanent fixture. Seeking creative independence, she left the group in <strong>1982</strong> alongside Nicky and Roma Ryan.</p>

<blockquote>
    <p>I joined my family's band, Clannad, as a teenager in 1980 to sing harmonies and play keyboards, but it wasn't musically challenging for me; I felt like an outsider. ... My split from Clannad two years later caused a conflict of loyalty for a time, because I went off with their manager Nicky Ryan, who had asked me to join Clannad in the first place.</p>
    <em>Enya</em>
</blockquote>

<h3 id="Solo_career">Solo career</h2>

<a class="new-amazon-album-link" href="/video-i-want-tomorrow/">
<Image src={iwt} alt="" width="300" height="300" /> Enya in the music video for "I Want Tomorrow", from The Celts documentary.
</div>

<p>Enya recorded two early solo instrumental tracks, "An Ghaoth Ón Ghrian" ("The Solar Wind") and <a href="/watermark/#Miss_Claire_Remembers">"Miss Clare Remembers"</a>.</p> 

<p>These debuted on a 1983 demo compilation tape titled <em>Touch Travel</em>.</p> 

<p>She received her first solo credit as <strong>Enya</strong> (adapting the phonetic spelling of Eithne) for her soundtrack work on the 1984 film <em>The Frog Prince</em>.</p> 

<p>In 1987, Enya provided a spoken Gaelic reading of Psalms 91:11-13 for Sinéad O'Connor's debut album, <em>The Lion and the Cobra</em>, appearing on the track "Never Get Old".</p>

<blockquote>
    <p>Enya is more than just me. It's also Nicky, who arranges my melodies, and his wife Roma, who writes the lyrics. They believed in my music from day one.</p>
    <em>Enya</em>
</blockquote>

<p>Enya's major solo stepping stone came when she was commissioned to score the 1986 <a href="/the-celts-documentary/">BBC television documentary <em>The Celts</em></a>.</p> 

<p>This evocative music became her first solo studio album, <a href="/enya-album/"><em>Enya</em> (1987)</a>, released by BBC Records, though it initially attracted little mainstream attention.</p>

<h3 id="International_Breakthrough">International breakthrough</h2>

<p>Enya's true international breakthrough arrived in <strong>1988</strong> after she signed with Warner Music UK.</p> 

<p>She released the landmark album <a href="/watermark/"><em>Watermark</em></a>, heavily driven by the surprise hit <a href="/watermark/#Orinoco_Flow">"Orinoco Flow"</a>.</p> 

<p>The single unexpectedly topped the UK charts, and the album ultimately sold over <strong>8 million copies</strong>.</p>

<blockquote>
    <p>The success of Watermark surprised me. I never thought of music as something commercial; it was something very personal to me... The writing of a melody is an emotional moment; success doesn't make it easy.</p>
    <em>Enya</em>
</blockquote>

<h3>Cementing Her Cinematic Sound</h3>

<p>Three years later, she returned with the massively successful <a href="/shepherd-moons/"><em>Shepherd Moons</em> (1991)</a>.</p> 

<p>The album sold over 10 million copies worldwide and earned Enya her first <strong>Grammy Award for Best New Age Album</strong>.</p> 

<p>Her atmospheric sound became highly sought after by Hollywood filmmakers.</p> 

<p>Key soundtrack placements during this era include:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>L.A. Story (1991)</strong>: Featured <a href="/watermark/#On_Your_Shore">"On Your Shore"</a>, <a href="/watermark/#Exile">"Exile"</a>, and <a href="/the-celts/#Epona">"Epona"</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Toys (1992)</strong>: Featured the rhythmic <a href="/shepherd-moons/#Ebud%E6">"Ebudae"</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Green Card (1990)</strong>: Featured <a href="/watermark/#River">"River"</a>, <a href="/watermark/#Watermark">"Watermark"</a>, and <a href="/watermark/#Storms_In_Africa">"Storms In Africa"</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Far and Away (1992)</strong>: Featured <a href="/enya-other/#Book_Of_Days_English_Lyric">"Book Of Days"</a>.</li>
</ul>

<p>In 1992, Warner released a digitally remastered version of her debut album, renaming it <a href="/the-celts/"><em>The Celts</em></a>.</p> 

<p>Four years later, she released her fourth studio album, <a href="/the-memory-of-trees/"><em>The Memory of Trees</em> (1995)</a>, which also won a Grammy.</p>

<p>In 1997, Enya celebrated her first decade of success with a greatest hits collection, <a href="/paint-the-sky-with-stars/"><em>Paint The Sky With Stars: The Best of Enya</em></a>.</p> 

<p>This compilation featured two brand new tracks: <a href="/paint-the-sky-with-stars/#Paint_the_Sky_with_Stars">"Paint the Sky With Stars"</a> and <a href="/paint-the-sky-with-stars/#Only_If...">"Only If..."</a>.</p>

<h3 id="Return_and_A_Day_Without_Rain">Return and <em>A Day Without Rain</em></h2>

<p>Following a deliberate five-year break, Enya released <a href="/a-day-without-rain/"><em>A Day Without Rain</em></a> in late <strong>2000</strong>.</p> 

<p>Peaking at <strong>#2 on the US Billboard 200</strong>, it remains Enya's most commercially successful album to date.</p> 

<p>The album's emotional depth resonated deeply following the <strong>September 11, 2001 attacks</strong>.</p> 

<p>Her poignant track <a href="/a-day-without-rain/#Only_Time">"Only Time"</a> became the unofficial anthem for media coverage surrounding the tragedy.</p> 

<p>Though she initially frowned upon bootlegged remixes featuring news soundbites, Enya eventually released an official charity mix of "Only Time" to raise funds for 9/11 widows and children.</p>

<p>The somber association with 9/11 often clashed with her other projects at the time.</p> 

<p>During a 2002 CNN appearance, producers overlayed images of war while she performed "<a href="/enya-other/#May_It_Be">May It Be</a>", a song written specifically for <a href="/the-lord-of-the-rings/"><em>The Lord Of The Rings: The Fellowship Of The Ring</em></a>.</p>

<blockquote>
    <p>There is no formula to it because writing every song, for me, is a little journey. The first note has to lift you and make you go, 'What's this?' You play C, but why is it that one day it leads to G and it didn't yesterday? I don't know. It's everything. It's the walk you take in the morning, it's the night before, the meeting with people, landscapes, the chats, all of that evolves in some way into melody, but I'm not sure how it's going to happen. I'm dealing with the unknown all the time and that is exciting.</p>
    <em>Enya</em>
</blockquote>

<p>Because she records every vocal layer manually, Enya is a self-admittedly slow worker.</p> 

<p>Fans often endure waits of up to five years between new studio albums.</p> 

<p>In September 2004, a new song called <a href="/enya-amarantine/#Sumiregusa_Wild_Violet">"Sumiregusa" ("Wild Violet")</a> debuted in a Japanese Panasonic commercial.</p> 

<p>Warner Music Japan mistakenly announced an upcoming November album release, which Enya's team quickly retracted via her official website.</p>

<h3 id="1998">1998–2007: A Day Without Rain and Amarantine</h2>

<p>Her sixth studio album, <a href="/enya-amarantine/"><em>Amarantine</em></a>, was finally released in <strong>November 2005</strong>.</p> 

<p>The album was a critical success, winning the <strong>2007 Grammy Award for Best New Age Album</strong>.</p> 

<p>Enya followed this by releasing several Christmas-themed recordings in 2006.</p> 

<p>The EP <em>Sounds of the Season</em> was released in the United States via a partnership with NBC and Target.</p> 

<p>It featured classic carols like "Adeste Fideles" and "We Wish You a Merry Christmas," alongside two original tracks, "Christmas Secrets" and "The Magic of the Night".</p>

<blockquote>
    <p>Amarantine is an ancient word, which means eternity. The poets describe an undying flower with that word, and I fell in love with that idea ... For me that word suited rhythmically to the refrain of the piece. A-ma-ran-ti-ne. In our website the fans tried to guess something super-romantic to be the album's name, but this word was obviously never guessed by anyone.</p>
    <em>Enya</em>
</blockquote>

<p>By late November 2006, special editions of <em>Amarantine</em> hit global markets.</p> 

<p>North America received <a href="/enya-other/"><em>Amarantine - Special Christmas Edition</em></a>, featuring a bonus disc of holiday tracks.</p> 

<p>The UK edition included postcards and a copy of Roma Ryan's companion book, <em>Water Shows the Hidden Heart</em>.</p> 

<p>Canadian fans could purchase a standalone EP titled <a href="/enya-other/"><em>Christmas Secrets</em></a>.</p>

<div class="new-amazon-album-link">
<Image src={grammy200705} alt="" width="300" height="300" />
Enya in 2007 Grammy Awards
</div>

<p>By mid-2007, Enya's total worldwide sales surpassed <strong>70 million albums</strong>.</p> 

<p>Her incredible financial success without ever touring prompted a US businessperson to coin the term <strong>"enyanomics"</strong>.</p> 

<p>In the summer of 2007, she received two major academic honors.</p> 

<p>She was awarded an honorary <strong>Doctor of Music</strong> from the National University of Ireland, Galway on June 29, followed by an honorary <strong>Doctor of Letters</strong> from the University of Ulster on July 10.</p>

<div style="clear:both"></div>

<h2 id="Recent_Releases">Recent Releases</h2>

<a class="new-amazon-album-link" href="/enya-amarantine/">
  <Image src={elEnya} alt="" title="Show bigger image" width="300" height="300" />
    Enya in a promotional photo for the Amarantine album.
</a>

<p>Enya initially planned to record an album of standard Christmas hymns for late 2007.</p> 

<p>Instead, the project evolved into a fully-fledged winter-themed studio album, <strong><em>And Winter Came...</em> (2008)</strong>.</p> 

<p>The album included the tribute track "My! My! Time Flies!", dedicated to the late Irish guitarist Jimmy Faulkner.</p> 

<p>This track marked the first time a prominent guitar solo was used on an Enya song since her 1987 debut.</p> 

<p><em>And Winter Came...</em> sold almost <strong>3.5 million copies worldwide</strong> by 2011.</p>

<h3>A Long Break and Viral Resurgence</h3>

<p>Following a grueling promotional schedule, Enya took an extended hiatus.</p> 

<p>She rested, visited relatives in Australia, and renovated a new property in the south of France.</p> 

<p>In November 2009, Warner released a comprehensive CD/DVD package, <strong><em>The Very Best of Enya</em></strong>.</p> 

<p>In <strong>2013</strong>, her hit "Only Time" experienced a massive viral resurgence.</p> 

<p>Volvo Trucks used the song in an "Epic Split" advertisement featuring actor Jean-Claude Van Damme.</p> 

<p>The commercial triggered a wave of YouTube parodies, pushing "Only Time" back onto the US Billboard Hot 100 at No. 43.</p>

<blockquote>
    <p>I felt I didn't know what to do ... I thought I needed a break, and the music needed a break ... I know it sounds like a long time, but to me, it felt like six months.</p>
    <em>Enya</em>
</blockquote>

<h3>Return to the Dark Sky</h3>

<p>Enya finally returned to Aigle Studios in 2012 to record her eighth album, <strong><em>Dark Sky Island</em></strong>.</p> 

<p>Released in November 2015, the album was inspired by Roma Ryan's poetry about the island of Sark, the world's first designated dark-sky preserve.</p> 

<p>The album's lead single, <strong>"Echoes in Rain"</strong>, premiered on UK radio in October 2015.</p> 

<p><em>Dark Sky Island</em> reached No. 4 in the UK and No. 8 in the US Billboard 200.</p> 

<p>During her promotional tour, she performed at the Universal Studios Japan Christmas show in Osaka.</p> 

<p>In December 2016, she made a rare television appearance on the RTE special <em>Christmas Carols from Cork</em>, her first Irish TV performance in over seven years.</p>

<h3 id="Musical_style_and_other_projects">Musical style and other projects</h2>

<blockquote>
    <p>Anytime I hear a song, it's the whole story of the song. You actually go into the moment, into that year. It's like a diary: going back to that day, where you had written it, worked on it, the life of it, the day you finished it. What you ate that day, the earrings you wore. Every song — every song is a punch in the gut.</p>
    <em>Enya</em>
</blockquote>

<p>Enya is celebrated for seamlessly blending languages to suit the emotional resonance of her melodies.</p> 

<p>While she predominantly sings in English, a significant portion of her catalog is recorded in <strong>Irish Gaelic</strong> and <strong>Latin</strong>.</p> 

<p>Her linguistic versatility extends to Welsh, Spanish, French, and Japanese.</p> 

<p>She has also recorded in fictional languages.</p> 

<p>For Peter Jackson's film <a href="/the-lord-of-the-rings/"><em>The Lord of the Rings: The Fellowship of the Ring</em> (2001)</a>, Enya sang <a href="/enya-other/#May_It_Be">"May It Be"</a> in English and <strong>Quenya</strong>, and <a href="/enya-other/#An%EDron">"Aníron"</a> entirely in <strong>Sindarin</strong>—both Elvish tongues created by J.R.R. Tolkien.</p> 

<p>On her 2005 album <a href="/enya-amarantine/"><em>Amarantine</em></a>, she utilized <strong>Loxian</strong>, a completely original language invented by her lyricist Roma Ryan.</p>

<p>While Enya frequently performs live on global television and award broadcasts, she has never mounted a full concert tour, though she considers the logistical hurdle a "great challenge" she might tackle one day.</p>

<h3 id="Favourites">Favourites</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Movies:</strong> Old black &amp; white classics, particularly Hitchcock's <em>Rebecca</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>Book:</strong> <a href="/the-lord-of-the-rings/"><em>The Lord of the Rings</em></a> by J.R.R. Tolkien. Her absolute favourite character is Frodo.</li>
    <li><strong>Music:</strong> Classical composers like Rachmaninoff and Debussy.<br />
    <em>"I was asked the question 'What was the first single you ever bought?' and I thought 'I have never bought a single or an album'. Even as a teenager! It's strange. I don't like listening to music. People find it hard to understand that."</em></li>
    <li><strong>Her own songs:</strong> Marble Halls, On Your Shore, Smaointe..., Fallen Embers, Amarantine, If I Could Be Where You Are.</li>
    <li><strong>Fashion designer:</strong> Maria Grachvogel, who famously designed <a href="/red.jpg" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">the red dress</a> Enya wore to the <em>Amarantine</em> launch party.</li>
    <li><strong>Food:</strong> Italian, French, and Indian cuisines.<br />
    <em>"I don't eat red meat. I eat fish and lots of vegetables. I've been told I'm a very good cook but take forever to prepare one meal. Everything has to be absolutely perfect and nobody is allowed into the kitchen."</em></li>
    <li><strong>Drink:</strong> Champagne and very dry white wine.</li>
    <li><strong>Pets:</strong> <em>"I don't have pets, I have two guard dogs."</em><br />
    <em>"I love cats, at one stage I had 12. It was just bliss. They'd all lie around in the sun and then come up and climb around my neck."</em></li>
</ul>

<h3 id="Trivia">Trivia</h2>

<ul>
    <li>An actual asteroid floating in space, <a href="https://cfa-www.harvard.edu/cfa/ps/special/rocknroll/0006433.html" target="_blank"><strong>"6433 Enya"</strong></a>, was discovered in 1978 and named in her honor.</li>
    <li>Enya co-owns a private recording facility named <strong>Aigle Studios</strong>. "Aigle" translates to "Eagle" in French.</li>
    <li>In her rare downtime, Enya's hobbies include collecting artwork, painting, reading, and watching romantic black-and-white films.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="awards">Awards</h2>

<p>Enya's critical acclaim is anchored by <strong>four Grammy Awards</strong>, all won in the <em>Best New Age Album</em> category:</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>1993:</strong> Won for <a href="/shepherd-moons/"><em>Shepherd Moons</em></a>.</li>
    <li><strong>1997:</strong> Won for <a href="/the-memory-of-trees/"><em>The Memory of Trees</em></a>.</li>
    <li><strong>2002:</strong> Won for <a href="/a-day-without-rain/"><em>A Day Without Rain</em></a>.</li>
    <li><strong>2007:</strong> Won for <a href="/enya-amarantine/"><em>Amarantine</em></a>.</li>
</ul>

<p>Her cinematic work has also drawn major award attention.</p> 

<p>Her song <a href="/enya-other/#May_It_Be">"May It Be"</a> for <a href="/the-lord-of-the-rings/"><em>The Lord Of The Rings - The Fellowship Of The Ring</em></a> received dual nominations for a Golden Globe and an <strong>Academy Award for Best Original Song</strong> in 2002.</p>

<p>Other major industry accolades include:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>IRMA Awards:</strong> Named "Best Female Irish Artist" in 1989 and 1993.</li>
    <li><strong>World Music Awards:</strong> Captured five distinct trophies, including "Best Solo Irish Artist", "Best New Age Artist", and "Best-selling Female Artist" across the 2001, 2002, and 2006 ceremonies.</li>
